      "id": 1,
      "text": "Seminary, Mississippi, grew around the 1845 Zion Seminary, a boarding school that taught law, medicine, and religious studies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seminary%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Civil War, all but one building of Zion Seminary burned, a loss that the town still remembers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seminary%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town was officially incorporated in 1899, choosing the name \"Seminary\" to honor the historic school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seminary%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Seminary sits in south‑central Covington County, with Okatoma Creek winding through its western edge.",
